# EFFICIENCY MASTERY: INFINITY PROTOCOL v6.2

> **Maximum Throughput**: Never block the main thread. Never await sequentially if you can await concurrently.

## 1. PARALLEL EXECUTION (Promise.all)
Convert sequential `await` cascades into parallel data fetching matrices to drastically reduce backend compute time.

**Anti-Pattern (Slow, Sequential Waterfall):**
```typescript
// Takes 300ms + 200ms = 500ms
const user = await getUser(userId);
const orders = await getOrders(userId);
```

**Infinity Protocol Standard (Fast, Concurrent):**
```typescript
// Takes Math.max(300ms, 200ms) = 300ms
const [user, orders] = await Promise.all([
  getUser(userId),
  getOrders(userId)
]);
```

## 2. BUNDLE INTEGRITY & TREE-SHAKING
- Enforce a **200KB** limit for the initial JS payload. 
- Use `next/dynamic` to lazy-load heavy components (PDF generators, charting libraries, rich text editors) that are not required for Above-The-Fold LCP.

**Functional Implementation (Lazy Loading):**
```tsx
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';

// Heavy chart only loads when the component actually renders on client
const LazyChart = dynamic(() => import('@/components/AnalyticsChart'), {
  ssr: false,
  loading: () => <div className="h-64 w-full animate-pulse bg-white/5 rounded-xl" />
});
```

## 3. AGENT RESOURCE ECONOMY
- Read max 3 files per turn unless explicitly critical — use `grep`/`find` to locate first.
- Never read `package-lock.json` or `yarn.lock` unless debugging exact version mismatches.
- Multi-step OS tasks (Build + Test + Deploy): execute sequentially, not in parallel, to preserve 16GB RAM constraints.

## 4. TERMINAL DISCIPLINE
- Long output commands: pipe to file or use status-only flags (`npm ci --quiet`). Never flood chat with 500-line success logs.
- Always use `set -euo pipefail` in bash scripts to fail fast and prevent runaway execution.

## Implementation Architecture: Concurrent Batch Execution Wrapper

When handling massive datasets (e.g., 5,000 Firestore documents), `Promise.all` straight across will blow up the stack or rate limits. Use this generic batcher function to process arrays concurrently up to a strict concurrency cap.

```typescript
// lib/utils/batcher.ts

/**
 * Executes async tasks concurrently up to a specified limit.
 * Crucial for bulk Firestore updates or API calls without hitting 429 schemas.
 */
export async function runConcurrentBatches<T, R>(
  items: T[],
  concurrencyLimit: number,
  iteratorFn: (item: T, index: number) => Promise<R>
): Promise<R[]> {
  const results: R[] = [];
  const executing: Promise<void>[] = [];
  
  for (const [index, item] of items.entries()) {
    const p = Promise.resolve().then(() => iteratorFn(item, index));
    results.push(p as unknown as R); // Store reference to final result
    
    // Create execution wrapper that auto-removes itself upon completion
    const e: Promise<void> = p.then(() => {
      executing.splice(executing.indexOf(e), 1);
    });
    
    executing.push(e);
    
    // Wait for a slot to free up if we've hit the ceiling
    if (executing.length >= concurrencyLimit) {
      await Promise.race(executing);
    }
  }
  
  return Promise.all(results);
}

// Usage Example: update 3000 records, 50 at a time
// await runConcurrentBatches(records, 50, async (doc) => db.update(doc.id, payload));
```
